/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A phase is a part of the system with uniform properties; π counts such parts.
   - Inside one phase the intensive variables are temperature T, pressure and the mole fractions.
   - The equations are the equilibrium conditions from 6.1.
   - F is the number of degrees of freedom, C the number of components, π the number of phases; the 2 stands for temperature and pressure.
   - Pure water with liquid and vapour present: C = 1, π = 2, so F = 1.
   - A reaction ties species together, so species are not components.
   - Most laboratory and plant equipment runs at a fixed pressure — a fume hood at 1 atm, an open beaker at 1 atm, or a reactor vented through a pressure-control valve set at 2 bar.
   - F is degrees of freedom, C components, π phases; the rule applies when either temperature or pressure is held fixed.
   - Water boiling in an open pan at 1 atm: C = 1, π = 2, so F = 1 − 2 + 1 = 0.
   - In a closed system of known overall composition, fixing any two independent variables fixes every remaining intensive variable and the amounts of all phases.
*/
