/* book exam traps: Counting species as components when a reaction exists. Three species with one independent reaction give C = 2, not 3. | Adding the full +2 when the pressure is already fixed. Once the pressure is set, use F = C − π + 1, otherwise every answer is one too high. | Remembering that F counts intensive variables only. Phase amounts are extensive and are not degrees of freedom; Duhem's theorem is what brings them in. | Missing extra constraints such as electroneutrality in an electrolyte solution, which reduce C by one more. | Thinking any two intensive variables will do for Duhem's theorem. They must be independent; fixing T and the boiling pressure of a pure liquid is a contradiction, not a specification. */
